Description.Explanation:
By default, Amazon DocumentDB clusters do not capture audit logs. This can allow malicious behavior to go undetected and inhibit forensic analysis in the event of a breach.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an Amazon DocumentDB cluster without audit logging.
<pre>
"Resources": {
      "DocDBParameterGroup001": {
        "Type": "AWS::DocDB::DBClusterParameterGroup",
        "Properties": {
            "Description": "docdb cluster test group",
            "Family": "docdb4.0",
            "Name": "test",
            "Parameters": {
                "audit_logs": "disabled"
            }
        }
    }
}</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
To enable Amazon DocumentDB audit logging, define and set the <code>audit_logs</code> parameter in your <code>Parameters</code> block to <code>enabled</code>.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following fixes the template in <code>Example 1</code> by enabling audit logging.
<pre>
"Resources": {
      "DocDBParameterGroup001": {
        "Type": "AWS::DocDB::DBClusterParameterGroup",
        "Properties": {
            "Description": "docdb cluster test group",
            "Family": "docdb4.0",
            "Name": "test",
            "Parameters": {
                "audit_logs": "enabled"
            }
        }
    }
}</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, Amazon Neptune clusters do not capture audit logs. This can allow malicious behavior to go undetected and inhibit forensic analysis in the event of a breach.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an Amazon Neptune cluster without audit logging.
<pre>
"Resources": {
      "NeptuneParameterGroup001": {
        "Type": "AWS::Neptune::DBClusterParameterGroup",
        "Properties": {
            "Description": "neptune cluster test group",
            "Family": "docdb4.0",
            "Name": "test",
            "Parameters": {
                "neptune_enable_audit_log": "0"
            }
        }
    }
}</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
To enable Amazon Neptune audit logging, define and set the <code>neptune_enable_audit_log</code> parameter in your <code>Parameters</code> block.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code> by enabling audit logging.
<pre>
"Resources": {
      "NeptuneParameterGroup001": {
        "Type": "AWS::Neptune::DBClusterParameterGroup",
        "Properties": {
            "Description": "neptune cluster test group",
            "Family": "docdb4.0",
            "Name": "test",
            "Parameters": {
                "neptune_enable_audit_log": "1"
            }
        }
    }
}</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Cross-Origin Resource Sharing, commonly referred to as CORS, is a technology that allows a domain to define a policy for its resources to be accessed by a web page hosted on a different domain. Historically, web browsers have restricted their domain resources from being accessed by scripts loaded from a different domain to abide by the same origin policy.
CORS provides a method for a domain to allow other domains and enable them to access its resources.

Be careful when defining a CORS policy because an overly permissive policy configured at the server level for a domain or a directory on a domain can expose more content for cross domain access than intended. CORS can enable a malicious application to communicate with a victim application inappropriately, which can lead to information disclosure, spoofing, data theft, relay, or other attacks.

Implementing CORS can increase an application's attack surface and should only be used when necessary.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an overly permissive CORS policy for an Azure SignalR web application.
<pre>
{
    ...
    "type": "Microsoft.SignalRService/SignalR",
    ...
    "properties": {
    ...
        "cors": {
            "allowedOrigins": ["*"]
        },
    ...
}
</pre>
<b>Example 2:</b> The following example template defines an overly permissive CORS policy for an Azure web application.
<pre>
{
    "apiVersion": "2020-12-01",
    "type": "Microsoft.Web/sites",
    ...
    "properties": {
        ...
        "siteConfig": {
            ...
            "cors": {
                "allowedOrigins": [
                    "*"
                ]
            },
    ...
}</pre>
<b>Example 3:</b> The following example template defines an overly permissive CORS policy for an Azure Maps account.
<pre>
{
    "apiVersion": "2021-12-01-preview",
    "type": "Microsoft.Maps/accounts",
    ...
    "properties":{
        "cors":{
            "allowedOrigins": ["*"]
        }
    },
    ...
}
</pre>
<b>Example 4:</b> The following example template defines an overly permissive CORS policy for an Azure Cosmos DB account.
<pre>
{
    "type": "Microsoft.DocumentDB/databaseAccounts",
    ...
    "properties": {
        "cors": [{
            "allowedOrigins":"*"
        }],
    ...
}
</pre>
<b>Example 5:</b> The following example template defines an overly permissive CORS policy for an Azure storage blob service.
<pre>
{
"type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/blobServices",
    ...
    "properties": {
        "cors": {
            "corsRules": [
                {
                    "allowedOrigins":["*"],
                    ...
                }
            ]
        }
    }
    ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Do not use a wildcard (<code>*</code>) as the value for the <code>allowedOrigins</code> property. Instead, provide an explicit list of trusted domains.

<b>Example 6:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code> by specifying an explicitly trusted domain for an Azure SignalR web application.
<pre>
{
    ...
    "type": "Microsoft.SignalRService/SignalR",
    ...
    "properties": {
    ...
        "cors": {
        "allowedOrigins": ["www.trusted.com"]
        },
    ...
}
</pre>
<b>Example 7:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 2</code> by specifying an explicitly trusted domain for an Azure web application.
<pre>
{
    "apiVersion": "2020-12-01",
    "type": "Microsoft.Web/sites",
    ...
    "properties": {
        ...
        "siteConfig": {
            ...
            "cors": {
                "allowedOrigins": [
                    "www.trusted.com"
                ]
            },
    ...
}
</pre>
<b>Example 8:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 3</code> by specifying an explicitly trusted domain for an Azure Maps account.
<pre>
{
    "apiVersion": "2021-12-01-preview",
    "type": "Microsoft.Maps/accounts",
    ...
    "properties":{
            "cors":{
                "allowedOrigins": ["www.trusted.com"]
            }
        },
    ...
}
</pre>
<b>Example 9:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 4</code> by specifying an explicitly trusted domain for an Azure Cosmos DB.
<pre>
{
    "type": "Microsoft.DocumentDB/databaseAccounts",
    ...
    "properties": {
        "cors": [{
            "allowedOrigins":"www.trusted.com"
        }],
    ...
}
</pre>
<b>Example 10:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 5</code> by specifying an explicitly trusted domain for an Azure blob Service.
<pre>
{
"type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/blobServices",
    ...
    "properties": {
        "cors": {
            "corsRules": [
                {
                    "allowedOrigins":["www.trusted.com"],
                    ...
                }
            ]
        }
    }
    ...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Azure SQL Database instances with unrestricted ranges of allowable IP addresses unnecessarily broaden an organization's attack surface. Services open to interaction with the public are subjected to almost continuous scanning and probing by attackers.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an overly exposed Azure SQL Database instance.
<pre>
{
    "resources": [
        {
            "name": "[variables('sqlServerName')]",
            "type": "Microsoft.Sql/servers",
            ...
            "resources": [
                {
                    "type": "databases",
                    ...
                },
                {
                    "name": "AllowAllIPs",
                    "type": "firewallrules",
                    "apiVersion": "2020-02-02-preview",
                    "location": "[parameters('location')]",
                    "properties": {
                        "endIpAddress": "255.255.255.255",
                        "startIpAddress": "0.0.0.0"
                    },
                }
            ]
        }
    ]
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Configure Azure SQL Database firewall rules to accept connections from a restricted range of IP addresses.

To specify trusted sources, configure the <code>firewallrules</code> resource with appropriate <code>startIpAddress</code> and <code>endIpAddress</code> values.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example template reduces the risk in <code>Example 1</code>. It restricts Azure SQL Database instance access to all Azure-internal IP addresses.
<pre>
{
    "resources": [
        {
            "name": "[variables('sqlServerName')]",
            "type": "Microsoft.Sql/servers",
            ...
            "resources": [
                {
                    "type": "databases",
                    ...
                },
                {
                    "name": "AllowAllIPs",
                    "type": "firewallrules",
                    "apiVersion": "2020-02-02-preview",
                    "location": "[parameters('location')]",
                    "properties": {
                        "endIpAddress": "0.0.0.0",
                        "startIpAddress": "0.0.0.0"
                    },
                }
            ]
        }
    ]
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Insufficient access configurations expose systems and broaden an organization's attack surface. Services open to interaction with the internet are subject to continuous scanning and probing by malicious entities.

This is especially problematic when a zero-day exploit for an exposed service is discovered and published (e.g Heartbleed). Attackers can actively pursue and search for unpatched and exposed systems to exploit.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an Azure Container Registry with unrestricted network access by enabling the property <code>publicNetworkAccess</code> and making no IP restrictions.
<pre>
{
    "name": "[variables('acrName')]",
    "type": "Microsoft.ContainerRegistry/registries",
    ...
    "properties": {
        "publicNetworkAccess": "Enabled",
        ..
}
</pre>
<b>Example 2:</b> The following example template defines an Azure Container Registry with unrestricted network access by specifying a broad allow list for the <code>networkRuleSet</code> property.
<pre>
{
    "name": "[variables('acrName')]",
    "type": "Microsoft.ContainerRegistry/registries",
    ...
    "properties": {
        "publicNetworkAccess": "Enabled",
        "networkRuleSet":
        {
            "defaultAction": "Allow",
            "ipRules":[{
                "action": "Allow",
                "value": "*"
            }]
        }
        ...
}</pre>

Systems protected by basic authentication offer attackers a potential weak link. Attackers can compromise passwords (especially weak ones) by brute-force, guessing, or social engineering.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a VM that allows SSH basic authentication.
<pre>
{
    "resources": [
        {
            "type": "Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines",
            "apiVersion": "2021-03-01",
            "name": "[variables('vmName')]",
            "location": "[parameters('location')]",
            ...
            "osProfile": {
                "computerName": "[variables('vmName')]",
                "adminUsername": "[parameters('adminUsername')]",
                "linuxConfiguration": {
                    "disablePasswordAuthentication": false
                }
            },
            ...
        }
    ]
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Use SSH key-based authentication to protect your systems from unauthorized access.

SSH keys are harder to compromise than traditional passwords due to the larger search-space that attackers would have to explore.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines a VM that must utilize SSH keys for authentication.
<pre>
{
    "resources": [
        {
            "type": "Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines",
            "apiVersion": "2021-03-01",
            "name": "[variables('vmName')]",
            ...
            "osProfile": {
                "computerName": "[variables('vmName')]",
                "adminUsername": "[parameters('adminUsername')]",
                "linuxConfiguration": {
                    "disablePasswordAuthentication": true,
                    "ssh": {
                        "publicKeys": [
                            {
                                "path": "[concat('/home/', parameters('adminUsername'), '/.ssh/authorized_keys')]",
                                "keyData": "[parameters('adminSSHKey')]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                }
            },
            ...
        }
    ]
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) clusters do not send log events to Azure Monitor. This can allow malicious behavior to go undetected and prevent forensic analysis in the event of a breach.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template shows an AKS cluster that does not send log events to Azure Monitor.
<pre>
{
    "name": "[split(parameters('aksResourceId'),'/')[8]]",
    "type": "Microsoft.ContainerService/managedClusters",
    "apiVersion": "2018-03-31",
    "properties": {
        "mode": "Incremental",
        "id": "[parameters('aksResourceId')]",
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
To make Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) clusters send log events to Azure Monitor, enable the log analytics agent using the <code>addonProfiles/omsagent</code> property. Note that this setting is only available for <code>apiVersion</code> later than 2017-08-31.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines the required <code>addonProfiles</code> property.
<pre>
{
    "name": "[split(parameters('aksResourceId'),'/')[8]]",
    "type": "Microsoft.ContainerService/managedClusters",
    "apiVersion": "2018-03-31",
    "properties": {
        "mode": "Incremental",
        "id": "[parameters('aksResourceId')]",
        "addonProfiles": {
        "omsagent": {
            "enabled": true,
            "config": {
                "logAnalyticsWorkspaceResourceID": "[parameters('workspaceResourceId')]"
            }
            }
        }
    }
    }
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Debugging interfaces provide attackers with information that they can leverage for a more targeted attack.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an Azure App Service with remote debugging enabled.
<pre>
{
    ...
    "type": "Microsoft.Web/sites/config",
    "properties":
    {
        ...
        "remoteDebuggingEnabled": true,
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Ensure that all debugging interfaces and related ports are disabled for production deployments.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines an Azure App Service with remote debugging disabled.
<pre>
{
    ...
    "type": "Microsoft.Web/sites/config",
    "properties":
    {
        ...
        "remoteDebuggingEnabled": false,
    }
}
</pre>

Ensure that Azure storage accounts require secure connections from clients by setting <code>supportsHttpsTrafficOnly</code> to <code>true</code>. Note that omitting this setting in templates prior to <code>apiVersion</code> 2019-04-01 defaults to allowing insecure connections to Azure storage accounts.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It explicitly configures the Azure storage account to only accept requests over a secure channel.
<pre>
{
    "name": "Storage1",
    "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts",
    "apiVersion": "2021-02-01",
    ...
    "properties": {
        "supportsHttpsTrafficOnly": true
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Sensitive Azure ARM template parameters, such as passwords, typically use the <code>securestring</code> or <code>secureobject</code> types.

For parameters that use a secure type, the parameter values are not logged or stored in the deployment history.

However, if a hardcoded default value is configured for a secure type, that default value is readable to anyone who can access the template or the deployment history.

Hard-coded secrets can compromise security in a way that is not easy to remedy. After the software is in production, an update is required to change the compromised secret.

In the case of a secret that is used to encrypt data, key-rotation steps must be administered to decrypt and re-encrypt all the data protected by the compromised key.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following template shows a parameter with a secret type that has a hardcoded default value.
<pre>
{
    ...
    "parameters": {
        "rootPassword": {
            "defaultValue": "HardcodedPassword",
            "type": "secureString"
        },
        "adminLogin": {
            "type": "string"
        },
        "sqlServerName": {
            "type": "string"
        }
    },
    ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
ARM template parameters that use <code>securestring</code> or <code>secureobject</code> types should not have default values.

If a default value is required at deployment time, consider using the <code>newGuid</code> function. Note that the <code>newGuid</code> function will be re-evaluated in subsequent re-deployments of the ARM template.

If the generated secret needs to be used across re-deployments, for example when using it to encrypt persistent data, consider integrating the ARM template with Azure Key Vault.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It shows a parameter with a secret type that is assigned a deployment-time generated GUID.
<pre>
{
    ...
    "parameters": {
        "rootPassword": {
            "defaultValue": "[newGuid()]",
            "type": "secureString"
        },
        "adminLogin": {
            "type": "string"
        },
        "sqlServerName": {
            "type": "string"
        }
    },
    ...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
A publicly accessible Kubernetes API server can expose the organization to attack.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a template that defines a Kubernetes service cluster that does not restrict the range of IP addresses that are allowed to connect to the API server.
<pre>
{
    "name": "TestCluster",
    "type": "Microsoft.ContainerService/managedClusters",
    "apiVersion": "2020-02-01",
    "location": "[resourceGroup().location]",
    "properties": {
    ...
    "servicePrincipalProfile": {
        "clientId": "422313d8-123a-41ea-8f8e-90821ff61c05",
        "secret": "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
    },
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Ensure that the Kubernetes API server is only accessible to legitimate or authorized entities by specifying the range of IP addresses that can connect to it.

Configure the appropriate property depending on the API version of your Azure Resource Manager template.

Templates with an <code>apiVersion</code> later than 2019-06-01 must configure the <code>apiServerAccessProfile</code> property map to contain the <code>authorizedIPRanges</code> property array of IP addresses.

Templates earlier than or equal to 2019-06-01 must use the <code>apiServerAuthorizedIPRanges</code> property, and templates earlier than 2019-02-01 should be updated to use a more recent <code>apiVersion</code> that supports IP range restrictions.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines the appropriate property for restricting access to the Kubernetes API server.
<pre>
{
    "name": "TestCluster",
    "type": "Microsoft.ContainerService/managedClusters",
    "apiVersion": "2020-02-01",
    "location": "[resourceGroup().location]",
    "properties": {
    ...
    "servicePrincipalProfile": {
        "clientId": "422313d8-123a-41ea-8f8e-90821ff61c05",
        "secret": "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
    },
    "apiServerAccessProfile": {
        "authorizedIPRanges": [
        "10.1.0.1"
        ]
    }
    }
}
</pre>

<b>Example 3:</b> The following example template restricts access to the Kubernetes API server for <code>apiVersions</code> earlier or equal to 2019-06-01 and later than or equal to 2019-02-01.
<pre>
{
    "name": "AKSClus",
    "type": "Microsoft.ContainerService/managedClusters",
    "apiVersion": "2019-02-01",
    ...
    "properties": {
    ...
    "servicePrincipalProfile": {
        "clientId": "422313d8-123a-41ea-8f8e-90821ff61c05",
        "secret": "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
    },
    "apiServerAuthorizedIPRanges": [
        "192.168.0.1",
        "192.168.0.18"
    ]
    }
}
</pre>

Ensure Azure SQL server auditing is enabled and that the <code>AuditActionsAndGroups</code> includes the <code>SUCCESSFUL_DATABASE_AUTHENTICATION_GROUP</code>, <code>FAILED_DATABASE_AUTHENTICATION_GROUP</code>, and <code>BATCH_COMPLETED_GROUP</code> actions.

Note that an enable auditing on both server and database layers.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines an Azure SQL server instance with auditing enabled and includes the recommended <code>auditActionsAndGroups</code>.
<pre>
{
"type": "Microsoft.Sql/servers",
...
"resources":[
    {
        "name": "ProdSQLServer",
        "type": "Microsoft.Sql/servers",
        "apiVersion": "2021-02-01-preview",
        ...
        "properties": {
            "collation": "SQL_Latin1_General_CP1_CI_AS",
            ...
            "resources": [
            {
                "type": "auditingSettings",
                "apiVersion": "2021-02-01-preview",
                "properties": {
                    "state": "Enabled",
                    "auditActionsAndGroups": [ "DATABASE_LOGOUT_GROUP", "SUCCESSFUL_DATABASE_AUTHENTICATION_GROUP","FAILED_DATABASE_AUTHENTICATION_GROUP", "BATCH_COMPLETED_GROUP" ],
                    ...
                }
            }
            ]
        }
        ...
    }]
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, Amazon Redshift clusters are not publicly accessible.

Enabling public access to a cluster results in a wider attack surface for the organization.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template explicitly enables public access to an Amazon Redshift cluster.
<pre>
"Resources": {
    "RDCluster": {
      "Type": "AWS::Redshift::Cluster",
      "Properties": {
        "NodeType": "ds2.xlarge",
        "ClusterType": "single-node",
        "DBName": "rdb",
        "PubliclyAccessible": true,
        "MasterUsername": "rduser",
        "MasterUserPassword": {
          "Ref": "MasterUserPassword"
        }
      }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
To activate Amazon Redshift audit logging, define a <code>LoggingProperties</code> entity that specifies an Amazon S3 Bucket name where logs are stored.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following fixes the template in <code>Example 1</code> by enabling audit logging.
<pre>
"Resources": {
    "RedshiftClusterTest": {
      "Properties": {
        "NodeType": "dc1.large",
        "Port": 5439,
        "VpcSecurityGroupIds": [
          "${RedshiftSecurityGroup}"
        ],
        "ClusterSubnetGroupName": "RedshiftClusterSubnetGroup",
        "ClusterType": "single-node"
        "MasterUserPassword": "MasterUserPassword",
        "MasterUsername": "MasterUsername",
        "DBName": "${DatabaseName}",
        "IamRoles": ["RawDataBucketAccessRole.Arn"],
        "PubliclyAccessible": true
      },
      "Type": "AWS::Redshift::Cluster"
      "KmsKeyId": "K213",
        "ClusterSubnetGroupName": "RedshiftClusterSubnetGroup",
        "LoggingProperties": {
          "BucketName": "RedshiftClusterLogs"
        }
      }
    }
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, Amazon Redshift clusters do not require transport encryption. This exposes the data to unauthorized access, tampering, and potential theft.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an Amazon Redshift cluster that does not enforce transport security.
<pre>
{
    "Resources": {
        "RedShiftParms": {
            "Type": "AWS::Redshift::clusterParameterGroup",
            "Properties": {
                "Description": "parameter group",
                "ParameterGroupFamily": "redshift-1.0",
                "Parameters": [
                    {
                        "ParameterName": "require_ssl",
                        "ParameterValue": "false"
                    }
                ]
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Enforce transport security for communication with Amazon Redshift clusters using the <code>require_ssl</code> parameter.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code> by defining an Amazon Redshift cluster that requires SSL communication with clients.
<pre>
{
    "Resources": {
        "RedShiftParms": {
            "Type": "AWS::Redshift::clusterParameterGroup",
            "Properties": {
                "Description": "parameter group",
                "ParameterGroupFamily": "redshift-1.0",
                "Parameters": [
                    {
                        "ParameterName": "require_ssl",
                        "ParameterValue": "true"
                    }
                ]
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Secrets with long validity periods are prone to theft and consequently place the organization at risk of breach.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a template that defines a key without an expiration date.
<pre>
  {
    "type": "Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults/secrets",
    "name": "[concat(parameters('keyVaultName'), '/', parameters('secretName'))]",
    "apiVersion": "2019-09-01",
    "location": "[parameters('location')]",
    "dependsOn": [
        "[resourceId('Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults', parameters('keyVaultName'))]"
    ],
    "properties": {
        "value": "[parameters('secretValue')]"
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Ensure that secrets are valid for a limited period of time by configuring an expiration date. Shorter validity periods ensure that secrets are rotated on a regular basis, thereby reducing the likelihood of compromise and the length of time that a compromised secret is in use.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It declares a secret and defines an expiration date for it.
<pre>
{
    "type": "Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults/secrets",
    "name": "[concat(parameters('keyVaultName'), '/', parameters('secretName'))]",
    "apiVersion": "2019-09-01",
    "location": "[parameters('location')]",
    "dependsOn": [
        "[resourceId('Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults', parameters('keyVaultName'))]"
    ],
    "properties": {
        "value": "[parameters('secretValue')]",
        "attributes": {
            "enabled": "true",
            "exp": "1694589805"
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Custom Azure subscription owner roles provide unrestricted access to all resources under the subscription, greatly increasing the exposure of the organization to risk and violating the least privilege principle.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a custom owner role definition with all possible privileges.
<pre>
{
    "resources": [
        {
            "name": "roleDef",
            "type": "Microsoft.Authorization/roleDefinitions",
            "apiVersion": "2018-01-01-preview",
            "properties": {
                "roleName": "InfraAdmin",
                "description": "Infrastructure Admin",
                "permissions": [
                    {
                        "actions": [
                            "*"
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                ...
            }
        }
    ]
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Ensure that custom roles have clearly defined duties and that they are granted the least privileges required to accomplish those duties.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines a custom role with constrained privileges.
<pre>
{
    "resources": [
        {
            "name": "roleDef",
            "type": "Microsoft.Authorization/roleDefinitions",
            "apiVersion": "2018-01-01-preview",
            "properties": {
                "roleName": "InfraMonitorOps",
                "description": "Infrastructure Operator Monitoring",
                "permissions": [
                    {
                        "actions": [
                            "Microsoft.Storage/*/read",
                            "Microsoft.Network/*/read",
                            "Microsoft.Compute/*/read",
                            "Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/start/action",
                            "Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/restart/action",
                            "Microsoft.Authorization/*/read",
                            "Microsoft.ResourceHealth/availabilityStatuses/read",
                            "Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/resourceGroups/read",
                            "Microsoft.Insights/alertRules/*",
                            "Microsoft.Insights/diagnosticSettings/*",
                            "Microsoft.Support/*"
                        ],
                        "assignableScopes": [
                            "/subscriptions/{subscriptionId1}",
                            "/subscriptions/{subscriptionId2}",
                            "/providers/Microsoft.Management/managementGroups/{groupId1}"
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                ...
            }
        }
    ]
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Unencrypted communication channels are prone to eavesdropping and tampering.

Serving web applications over HTTP allows attackers to perform man-in-the-middle attacks, giving them access to read or modify the data in transit over the channel.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a template that defines an Azure App Service that does not enforce HTTPS communication.
<pre>
{
    "resources": [
        {
            "name": "webSite",
            "type": "Microsoft.Web/sites",
            "apiVersion": "2020-12-01",
            "location": "location1",
            "tags": {},
            "properties": {
                "enabled": true
            },
            "resources": []
        }
    ]
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Enforce transport encryption to ensure that all data exchange is performed over encrypted channels and inhibit man-in-the-middle attacks.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It enforces HTTPS communication by enabling the <code>httpsOnly</code> property.
<pre>
{
    "resources": [
        {
            "name": "webSite",
            "type": "Microsoft.Web/sites",
            "apiVersion": "2020-12-01",
            "location": "location1",
            "tags": {},
            "properties": {
            "enabled": true,
                "httpsOnly": true
            },
            "resources": []
        }
    ]
}
</pre>

Encryption at rest is a vital control against data breaches. Whenever an S3 bucket is created, enable encryption by default and specify a valid encryption key. The key can either be automatically generated by S3 or an existing one stored in the AWS Key Management Service.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code requests that S3 encrypt objects using an S3-managed key
<pre>...
"Resources": {
    "S3KeyEncryptedS3": {
        "Type": "AWS::S3::Bucket",
        "Properties": {
        "BucketEncryption": {
        "ServerSideEncryptionConfiguration": [
            { "ServerSideEncryptionByDefault": { "SSEAlgorithm": "AES256" }}
        ]
    }
}
...</pre>
<b>Example 2:</b> The following code requests that S3 encrypt objects using a KMS-managed key
<pre>...
"Resources": {
    "KmsKeyEncryptedS3": {
        "Type": "AWS::S3::Bucket",
        "Properties": {
        "BucketEncryption": {
        "ServerSideEncryptionConfiguration": [
            { "ServerSideEncryptionByDefault": { "SSEAlgorithm": "aws:kms", "KMSMasterKeyID": "a-valid-key-ARN" }}
        ]
    }
}
...</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, Amazon RDS instances are not encrypted. This exposes the data to potential theft and unauthorized access.

<pre>
"Resources": {
    "RDSDBExample": {
        "Type": "AWS::RDS::DBInstance",
        "Properties": {
            "StorageEncrypted": false,
            "DBName": "Test DB",
            "DBInstanceClass": "db.m4.large"
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Fortify recommends that you enable RDS encryption to protect the data at rest. Amazon RDS encryption uses the industry standard AES-256 encryption algorithm to encrypt your data on the server that hosts your DB instance.

<pre>
"Resources": {
    "RDSDBExample": {
        "Type": "AWS::RDS::DBInstance",
        "Properties": {
            "StorageEncrypted": true,
            "DBName": "Test DB",
            "DBInstanceClass": "db.m4.large"
        }
    }
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Loose access configurations unnecessarily expose systems and broaden an organization's attack surface. Services open to interaction with the public are subjected to almost continuous scanning and probing by malicious entities.

This is especially problematic when a zero-day exploit for an exposed service is discovered and published (e.g Heartbleed). Attackers can actively pursue and search for unpatched and exposed systems to exploit.

<pre>
"DbSecurity": {
  "Type": "AWS::RDS::DBSecurityGroup",
  "Properties": {
      "GroupDescription": "Ingress for Amazon RDS security group",
      "DBSecurityGroupIngress": {
          "CIDRIP": "0.0.0.0/0"
      }
  }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Define AWS Security Groups to restrict access to services and make those services only available to as limited an audience as possible.

For example, do not make ports that are typically used for management activities of services publicly accessible. Make sure these ports are only available to a restricted pool of systems that require authorized access.

<pre>
"DBSecurityGroup": {
  "Type": "AWS::RDS::DBSecurityGroup",
  "Properties": {
      "DBSecurityGroupIngress": {
          "EC2SecurityGroupId": {
              "Fn::GetAtt": [
                  "InstanceSecurityGroup",
                  "GroupId"
              ]
          }
      }
  }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Loose access configurations unnecessarily expose systems and broaden an organization's attack surface. Services open to interaction with the public are subjected to almost continuous scanning and probing by malicious entities.

This is especially problematic when a zero-day exploit for an exposed service is discovered and published (e.g Heartbleed). Attackers can actively pursue and search for unpatched and exposed systems to exploit.

<pre>
"PSQLSecurityGroup": {
    "Type": "AWS::EC2::SecurityGroup",
    "Properties": {
        "VpcId": {
            "Ref": "VpcId"
        },
        "GroupDescription": "Enable PSQL access via port 5432 to world",
        "SecurityGroupIngress": [
            {
                "CidrIp": "0.0.0.0/0",
                "FromPort": 5432,
                "IpProtocol": "tcp",
                "ToPort": 5432
            }
        ]
    }
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Define AWS Security Groups to restrict access to services and make those services only available to as limited an audience as possible.

For example, do not make ports that are typically used for management activities of services publicly accessible. Make sure these ports are only available to a restricted pool of systems that require authorized access.

<pre>
"PSQLSecurityGroup": {
    "Type": "AWS::EC2::SecurityGroup",
    "Properties": {
        "VpcId": {
            "Ref": "VpcId"
        },
        "GroupDescription": "Enable PSQL access via port 5432 to admin subnet only",
        "SecurityGroupIngress": [
            {
                "CidrIp": {
                    "Ref": "DbAdminSecGroup"
                },
                "FromPort": 5432,
                "IpProtocol": "tcp",
                "ToPort": 5432
            }
        ]
    }
</pre>

To block public access of S3 data and to forbid anyone from adding policies or ACLs that grant public access, under S3 property of <code>PublicAccessBlockConfiguration</code>, assign <code>true</code> to all of the following settings:

<code>BlockPublicAcls</code>
<code>IgnorePublicAcls</code>
<code>BlockPublicPolicy</code>
<code>RestrictPublicBuckets</code>

<b>Example 1:</b>An S3 bucket with all S3 Block Public Access settings.

<pre>...
"Resources": {
    "AWSS3Bucket": {
        "Type": "AWS::S3::Bucket",
            "Properties": {
                "PublicAccessBlockConfiguration": {
                    "BlockPublicAcls" : true,
                    "IgnorePublicAcls" : true,
                    "BlockPublicPolicy" : true,
                    "RestrictPublicBuckets" : true
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
...</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, SNS topics are not encrypted at rest. This potentially exposes the data to unauthorized viewers.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template fails to encrypt the SNS topic by neglecting to define an AWS KMS key identifier.
<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Description": "My SNS topic",
    "Resources": {
        "MySNSTopic": {
            "Type": "AWS::SNS::Topic",
            "Properties": {
                "Subscription": [
                    {
                        "Endpoint": "MySNSEndpoint",
                        "Protocol": "sqs"
                    }
                ],
                "TopicName": "MyTopic"
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Ensure that SNS topics are encrypted at rest to protect any confidential data exchanged over the service.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example template successfully defines an AWS KMS key identifier.
<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Description": "My SNS topic",
    "Resources": {
        "MySNSTopic": {
            "Type": "AWS::SNS::Topic",
            "Properties": {
                "Subscription": [
                    {
                        "Endpoint": "MySNSEndpoint",
                        "Protocol": "sqs"
                    }
                ],
                "TopicName": "MyTopic",
                "KmsMasterKeyId": "myKeyID"
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, EBS volumes are not encrypted.
<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template exposes the data to potential theft and unauthorized access.

<pre>
"Resources": {
    "EBSVOLExample": {
        "Type": "AWS::EC2::Volume",
        "Properties": {
            "Encrypted": false,
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Fortify recommends that you enable EBS encryption to protect the data at rest. Amazon EBS encryption uses the industry standard AES-256 encryption algorithm to encrypt your data on the server that hosts your DB instance.
<b>Example 2:</b> The following example template enables EBS encryption to protect the data at rest.

<pre>
"Resources": {
    "EBSVOLExample": {
        "Type": "AWS::EC2::Volume",
        "Properties": {
            "Encrypted": true,
        }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, Amazon ElastiCache Replication Groups are not encrypted. This exposes the data to unauthorized access and potential theft.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an Amazon ElastiCache Replication Group with encryption disabled.
<pre>
{
    "Description": "ElastiCache redis",
    "Resources": {
        "MyReplicationGroup": {
            "Type": "AWS::ElastiCache::ReplicationGroup",
            "Properties": {
                "AutomaticFailoverEnabled": true,
                "CacheNodeType": {
                    "Ref": "CacheNodeType"
                },
                Cache  "ParameterGroupName": {
                    "Ref": "CacheParameterGroup"
                },
                "CacheSubnetGroupName": {
                    "Ref": "CacheSubnetGroupName"
                },
                "Engine": "redis",
                "EngineVersion": {
                    "Ref": "EngineVersion"
                },
                "NumNodeGroups": {
                    "Ref": "NumShards"
                },
                "ReplicasPerNodeGroup": {
                    "Ref": "NumReplicas"
                },
                "PreferredMaintenanceWindow": "sat:10:00-sat:08:00",
                "SecurityGroupIds": [
                    {
                        "Ref": "SecurityGroup"
                    }
                ],
                "SnapshotRetentionLimit": {
                    "Ref": "SnapshotRetentionLimit"
                },
                "SnapshotWindow": "00:00-03:00"
            },
            "UpdatePolicy": {
                "UseOnlineResharding": true
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Enable ElastiCache encryption to protect the data at rest. Amazon ElastiCache Replication Group encryption uses the industry standard AES-256 encryption algorithm to encrypt the data on the server that hosts your database instance.

Configure the <code>AtRestEncryptionEnabled</code> property to enable encryption-at-rest for Amazon ElastiCache Replication Groups.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example template defines an Amazon ElastiCache Replication Group with encryption.
<pre>
{
    "Description": "ElastiCache redis",
    "Resources": {
        "MyReplicationGroup": {
            "Type": "AWS::ElastiCache::ReplicationGroup",
            "Properties": {
                "AutomaticFailoverEnabled": true,
                "AtRestEncryptionEnabled": true,
                "CacheNodeType": {
                    "Ref": "CacheNodeType"
                },
                "CacheParameterGroupName": {
                    "Ref": "CacheParameterGroup"
                },
                "CacheSubnetGroupName": {
                    "Ref": "CacheSubnetGroupName"
                },
                "Engine": "redis",
                "EngineVersion": {
                    "Ref": "EngineVersion"
                },
                "NumNodeGroups": {
                    "Ref": "NumShards"
                },
                "ReplicasPerNodeGroup": {
                    "Ref": "NumReplicas"
                },
                "PreferredMaintenanceWindow": "sat:10:00-sat:08:00",
                "SecurityGroupIds": [
                    {
                        "Ref": "SecurityGroup"
                    }
                ],
                "SnapshotRetentionLimit": {
                    "Ref": "SnapshotRetentionLimit"
                },
                "SnapshotWindow": "00:00-03:00"
            },
            "UpdatePolicy": {
                "UseOnlineResharding": true
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default CloudWatch Log Groups retain logs indefinitely. An unset value indicates that organizational data handling polices have not been consulted in order to set appropriate configurations. This might mean that the organization will incur unnecessary expenses in storing and managing logging events that are no longer relevant.

An attacker can launch a Denial of Wallet (DoW) attack by persistently prompting spurious event-logging over an extended period of time, which can impact the organization financially.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a template that fails to define a retention policy.

<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Resources": {
        "MyLogGroup": {
            "Type": "AWS::Logs::LogGroup",
            "Properties": {
                "LogGroupName": "Service01LogGroup"
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Ensure that CloudWatch Log Groups are configured with an appropriate retention period.

It is important to understand organizational data and log management policies in order to reduce any financial overhead and any other related liabilities associated with storing and managing log data.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following template defines a retention policy of 100 days.

<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Resources": {
        "MyLogGroup": {
            "Type": "AWS::Logs::LogGroup",
            "Properties": {
                "LogGroupName": "Service01LogGroup",
                "RetentionInDays": 100
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
AWS API Gateway methods that are publicly accessible can expose the organization to attack.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines publicly accessible AWS API Gateway methods.

<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Resources": {
        "MyMethod0": {
            "Type": "AWS::ApiGateway::Method",
            "Properties": {
                "RestApiId": "MyAPIID",
                "ResourceId": "MyResourceID",
                "HttpMethod": "GET",
                "AuthorizationType": "NONE"
            }
        },
        "MyMethod1": {
            "Type": "AWS::ApiGateway::Method",
            "Properties": {
                "RestApiId": "MyAPIID",
                "ResourceId": "MyResourceID",
                "HttpMethod": "POST",
                "AuthorizationType": "NONE",
                "ApiKeyRequired": false
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Ensure that AWS API Gateway methods are only accessible to legitimate or authorized entities.

To reduce exposure, do one of the following:

- Define an <code>AuthorizationType</code>
- Declare that an API key is required with the <code>ApiKeyRequired</code> property

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example template defines restricted API Gateway methods.

<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Resources": {
        "MyMethod0": {
            "Type": "AWS::ApiGateway::Method",
            "Properties": {
                "RestApiId": "MyAPIID",
                "ResourceId": "MyResourceID",
                "HttpMethod": "GET",
                "AuthorizationType": "AWS_IAM"
            }
        },
        "MyMethod1": {
            "Type": "AWS::ApiGateway::Method",
            "Properties": {
                "RestApiId": "MyAPIID",
                "ResourceId": "MyResourceID",
                "HttpMethod": "POST",
                "AuthorizationType": "NONE",
                "ApiKeyRequired": true
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, Neptune DB clusters are not encrypted. This exposes the data to potential theft and unauthorized access by attackers.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template fails to enable encryption at rest.
<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Description": "My Neptune DB cluster",
    "Resources": {
        "NeptuneDBCluster": {
            "Type": "AWS::Neptune::DBCluster",
            "Properties": {
                "BackupRetentionPeriod": 10,
                "DBClusterIdentifier": "MyDBClusterIdentifier",
                "DBClusterParameterGroupName": "MyNeptuneDBClusterParameterGroup",
                "DBSubnetGroupName": "MyNeptuneDBSubnetGroup",
                "IamAuthEnabled": true,
                "Port": 8080,
                "PreferredBackupWindow": "NeptuneDBClusterPreferredBackupWindow",
                "PreferredMaintenanceWindow": "NeptuneDBClusterPreferredMaintenanceWindow"
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Fortify recommends that you enable Neptune DB cluster encryption to protect the data at rest.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example template successfully defines a Neptune DB cluster with encryption enabled for data at rest.
<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Description": "My Neptune DB cluster",
    "Resources": {
        "NeptuneDBCluster": {
            "Type": "AWS::Neptune::DBCluster",
            "Properties": {
                "BackupRetentionPeriod": 10,
                "DBClusterIdentifier": "MyDBClusterIdentifier",
                "DBClusterParameterGroupName": "MyNeptuneDBClusterParameterGroup",
                "DBSubnetGroupName": "MyNeptuneDBSubnetGroup",
                "IamAuthEnabled": true,
                "Port": 8080,
                "PreferredBackupWindow": "NeptuneDBClusterPreferredBackupWindow",
                "PreferredMaintenanceWindow": "NeptuneDBClusterPreferredMaintenanceWindow",
                "StorageEncrypted": true
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Unencrypted communication channels are prone to eavesdropping and tampering.

By default, ElastiCache replication group transport encryption is disabled. This exposes the data to unauthorized access, potential theft, and tampering.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an ElastiCache replication group without transport encryption enabled.
<pre>
{
    "Resources": {
        "ReplicationGroup": {
            "Properties": {
            "Engine": "redis",
            "EngineVersion": "EngineVersion",
            "ReplicasPerNodeGroup": "NumReplicas",
            "PreferredMaintenanceWindow": "sat:07:00-sat:08:00",
            "AtRestEncryptionEnabled": true,
            "CacheParameterGroupName": "CacheParameterGroup",
            "SecurityGroupIds": [
                "SecurityGroup"
            ],
            "SnapshotRetentionLimit": "SnapshotRetentionLimit",
            "CacheNodeType": "CacheNodeType",
            "CacheSubnetGroupName": "CacheSubnetGroupName",
            "NumNodeGroups": "NumShards",
            "SnapshotWindow": "00:00-03:00",
            "ReplicationGroupDescription": "AWS::StackName"
            },
            "UpdatePolicy": {
            "UseOnlineResharding": true
            },
            "DeletionPolicy": "Snapshot",
            "UpdateReplacePolicy": "Snapshot",
            "Type": "AWS::ElastiCache::ReplicationGroup"
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Fortify recommends that you enable ElastiCache encryption to protect the data in transit.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example addresses the issue in <code>Example 1</code> by defining an ElastiCache replication group with transport encryption enabled using the <code>TransitEncryptionEnabled</code> property.
<pre>
{
    "Resources": {
        "ReplicationGroup": {
            "Properties": {
            "Engine": "redis",
            "EngineVersion": "EngineVersion",
            "ReplicasPerNodeGroup": "NumReplicas",
            "PreferredMaintenanceWindow": "sat:07:00-sat:08:00",
            "AtRestEncryptionEnabled": true,
            "CacheParameterGroupName": "CacheParameterGroup",
            "SecurityGroupIds": [
                "SecurityGroup"
            ],
            "SnapshotRetentionLimit": "SnapshotRetentionLimit",
            "CacheNodeType": "CacheNodeType",
            "CacheSubnetGroupName": "CacheSubnetGroupName",
            "NumNodeGroups": "NumShards",
            "SnapshotWindow": "00:00-03:00",
            "TransitEncryptionEnabled": true,
            "ReplicationGroupDescription": "AWS::StackName"
            },
            "UpdatePolicy": {
            "UseOnlineResharding": true
            },
            "DeletionPolicy": "Snapshot",
            "UpdateReplacePolicy": "Snapshot",
            "Type": "AWS::ElastiCache::ReplicationGroup"
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Defining access keys for your AWS root user account places all your AWS resources at risk if this key is compromised.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template creates an access key for the root user account.
<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Resources": {
        "SecKeys": {
            "Type": "AWS::IAM::AccessKey",
            "Properties": {
            "UserName": "Root"
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Following the principle of least privilege, create a dedicated IAM user with the least rights required to perform the necessary activities.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example template creates an access key for a limited privilege user that is expected to perform a limited set of operations on the organization's AWS infrastructure.
<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
        "Resources": {
        "SecKeys": {
            "Type": "AWS::IAM::AccessKey",
            "Properties": {
            "UserName": "DBAdmin"
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

To enable Amazon S3 server access logging, add a <code>LoggingConfiguration</code> property, which defines where logs are stored and assigns a prefix for all log object keys as follows:

<pre>...
"Resources": {
    "S3Bucket": {
        "Type": "AWS::S3::Bucket",
        "Properties": {
            "LoggingConfiguration" : {
                "DestinationBucketName" : "someLoggingBucket",
                "LogFilePrefix" : "someLogPrefix"
            }
...</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, CloudTrail log file validation is disabled, which prevents investigators from asserting that there has been no external tampering with CloudTrail log files.

A direct result of this is that an attacker with the necessary privileges can perform harmful configuration changes and cover their tracks by modifying CloudTrail logs.

<b>Example 1</b>: The following is an example of a trail with log file validation disabled by setting <code>EnableLogFileValidation</code> to <code>false</code>. Omitting the property results in the default value set to <code>false</code> as well.

<pre>
"myTrail": {
    "DependsOn": [
        "BucketPolicy"
    ],
    "Type": "AWS::CloudTrail::Trail",
    "Properties": {
        "S3BucketName": {
            "Ref": "S3Bucket"
        },
        "IsLogging": true,
        "EnableLogFileValidation": false
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Fortify recommends that you enable CloudTrail log file validation to collect digest files, which can be used to confirm whether anyone has tampered with the log files.

<b>Example 2</b>: The following example enables validation by setting the <code>EnableLogFileValidation</code> to <code>true</code>.
<pre>
"myTrail": {
    "DependsOn": [
        "BucketPolicy"
    ],
    "Type": "AWS::CloudTrail::Trail",
    "Properties": {
        "S3BucketName": {
            "Ref": "S3Bucket"
        },
        "IsLogging": true,
        "EnableLogFileValidation": true
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
A wildcard configuration for lambda principals violates the least privilege principle and allows an attacker to invoke the lambda from any account.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines a wildcard lambda principal.
<pre>
{
    "Resources": {
        "LambdaPerm": {
            "Type": "AWS::Lambda::Permission",
            "Properties": {
            "SourceAccount": "AWS::AccountId",
            "SourceArn": "bucket.Arn",
            "FunctionName": "function.Arn",
            "Action": "lambda:InvokeFunction",
            "Principal": "*"
            }
        }
    },
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Description": "Lambda Permission"
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Explicitly define lambda principals to restrict invocation of the lambda function to authorized accounts and services.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example template defines a specific principal authorized to invoke the lambda.
<pre>
{
    "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
    "Description": "Lambda Permission",
    "Resources": {
        "LambdaPerm": {
            "Type": "AWS::Lambda::Permission",
            "Properties": {
            "FunctionName": "function.Arn",
            "Action": "lambda:InvokeFunction",
            "Principal": "s3.amazonaws.com",
            "SourceAccount": "AWS::AccountId",
            "SourceArn": "bucket.Arn"
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, Amazon DocumentDB instances are not encrypted. This exposes the data to unauthorized access and potential theft.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a template that defines an Amazon DocumentDB with encryption disabled.
<pre>
"Resources": {
    "DocDBDefault": {
        "Type": "AWS::DocDB::DBCluster",
        "Properties": {
            "MasterUsername": "name",
            "MasterUserPassword": "password"
        }
    }
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Enable DocumentDB encryption to protect the data at rest. Amazon DocumentDB encryption uses the industry standard AES-256 encryption algorithm to encrypt the data on the server that hosts your database instance.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example shows a template that defines an Amazon DocumentDB with encryption.
<pre>
"Resources": {
    "DocDBDefault": {
        "Type": "AWS::DocDB::DBCluster",
        "Properties": {
            "MasterUsername": "name",
            "MasterUserPassword": "password"
            "StorageEncrypted": true
        }
    }
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, Amazon Redshift clusters are not encrypted. This exposes the data to unauthorized access and potential theft.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following template defines a Redshift cluster with no encryption enabled.
<pre>
"Resources": {
    "RedshiftClusterTest": {
        "Type": "AWS::Redshift::Cluster",
        "Properties": {
            "DBName": "mydb",
            "MasterUsername": "master",
            "MasterUserPassword": "masterPass",
            "NodeType": "ds2.xlarge",
            "ClusterType": "single-node",
        }
    }
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Enable Redshift encryption to protect the data at rest.

<b>Example 2:</b>The following template defines a Redshift cluster with encryption enabled by configuring the <code>Encrypted</code> property.
<pre>
"Resources": {
    "RedshiftClusterTest": {
        "Type": "AWS::Redshift::Cluster",
        "Properties": {
            "DBName": "mydb",
            "MasterUsername": "master",
            "MasterUserPassword": "masterPass",
            "NodeType": "ds2.xlarge",
            "ClusterType": "single-node",
            "Encrypted": true
        }
    }
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
If a Compute Engine instance is created without a user-managed service account specification, Google Cloud assigns a default service account to the instance. The access rights of the default service account are often beyond what the instance needs to operate. This violates the least privilege principle.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that creates a Compute Engine instance without a user-managed service account specification. The <code>service_account</code> block is missing.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "instance-demo" {
    name = "name-demo"
    machine_type = "e2-micro"
    boot_disk {
    ...
    }
    network_interface {
    ...
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Attach a user-managed service account to a Compute Engine instance to provide credentials to applications running on the instance.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. Defined in the <code>service_account</code> block of the Compute Engine instance is a user-managed service account whose privileges can be granted on an as-needed basis.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "instance-demo" {
    name = "name-demo"
    machine_type = "e2-micro"
    boot_disk {
    ...
    }
    network_interface {
    ...
    }
    service_account {
        email  = "foobar.service.account@example.com"
        scope = []
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
The Terraform configuration sets the access scope of a service account to <code>cloud-platform</code>, which allows access to most of the Google Cloud APIs. This greatly expands the attack surface accessible to any compromised Compute Engine instance and violates the least privilege principle.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that sets the access scope (<code>scopes</code>) of the service account to <code>cloud-platform</code>.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "compute-instance-demo" {
    name         = "name-demo"
    machine_type = "e2-micro"
    ...
    service_account {
        email  = "foobar.service.account@example.com"
        scopes = ["cloud-platform"]
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Access scopes are the legacy method of specifying authorization. Remove the <code>cloud-platform</code> access scope from the Compute Engine instance service account. Grant IAM roles to the service account on an as-needed basis.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. The access scope list is empty and IAM policies grant access to needed Cloud APIs for the service account.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "compute-instance-demo" {
    name         = "name-demo"
    machine_type = "e2-micro"
    ...
    service_account {
        email  = "foobar.service.account@example.com"
        scopes = []
    }
}

data "google_iam_policy" "policy_demo" {
    binding {
        role = "roles/compute.instanceAdmin"
        members = [
            "serviceAccount:foobar.service.account@example.com"
        ]
    }
    ...
}
</pre>

Specify a bucket to receive usage and storage logs of the bucket.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It specifies a bucket to receive log objects in the <code>log_bucket</code> argument.
<pre>
resource "google_storage_bucket" "bucket-demo" {
    name     = "name-demo"
    location = "US"
    logging {
        log_bucket = "logs-bucket-demo"
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
DNSSEC prevents DNS spoofing by providing the ability to use digital signatures for DNS response validation. Any DNSSEC signing algorithm that uses SHA-1, however, is susceptible to an ever-growing risk of attack. SHA-1 is no longer considered a secure hash algorithm when used with digital signatures.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that enables the DNSSEC with the insecure signing algorithm <code>rsasha1</code>.
<pre>
resource "google_dns_managed_zone" "zone-demo" {
...
  dnssec_config {
    default_key_specs {
      algorithm = "rsasha1"
      ...
    }
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Use any of DNSSEC signing algorithms recommended by IETF RFC 8624 such as <code>ecdsap256sha256</code>.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It requires the use of the secure algorithm <code>ecdsap256sha256</code> for DNSSEC signing.
<pre>
resource "google_dns_managed_zone" "zone-demo" {
...
  dnssec_config {
    default_key_specs {
      algorithm = "ecdsap256sha256"
      ...
    }
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
The IAM-based access control reduces human errors and promotes efficiency. Enabling the OS Login permits the use of IAM roles to automate the lifecycle management of Linux accounts for accessing all Compute Engine instances in the same project or organization.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that disables the use of IAM roles to manage SSH access by setting <code>enable-oslogin</code> to <code>false</code> in the <code>metadata</code> argument.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "compute-instance-demo" {
  ...
  metadata = {
    enable-oslogin = false
    ...
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Use Compute Engine IAM roles to manage SSH access by enabling the OS Login.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It enables the use of IAM roles to manage SSH access by setting <code>enable-oslogin</code> to <code>true</code> in the <code>metadata</code> argument.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "compute-instance-demo" {
  ...
  metadata = {
    enable-oslogin = true
    ...
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, a Cloud SQL instance accepts unencrypted and unsecured connections. These connections expose data to unauthorized access, potential theft, and tampering.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that does not require a database instance to enforce SSL for all connections by setting <code>require_ssl</code> to <code>false</code>.
<pre>
resource "google_sql_database_instance" "database_instance_demo" {
  ...
  settings {
    ip_configuration {
      require_ssl = false
      ...
    }
    ...
  }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Enforce SSL for all connections. Do not rely on the default settings, which might change.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It requires the database instance to enforce SSL for all connections by setting <code>require_ssl</code> to <code>true</code> in the <code>ip_configuration</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_sql_database_instance" "database_instance_demo" {
  ...
  settings {
    ip_configuration {
      require_ssl = true
      ...
    }
    ...
  }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Database backups are critical to protect against data loss or corruption. Automated backups of a Cloud SQL database instance should be explicitly configured and enabled.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that disables database instance backup configurations by setting <code>enabled</code> to <code>false</code>.
<pre>
resource "google_sql_database_instance" "database_instance_demo" {
  ...
  settings {
    backup_configuration {
      enabled = false
      ...
    }
  }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Specify and enable database instance backup configurations. Do not rely on the default settings, which might change.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It specifies backup configurations (<code>backup_configuration</code>) and enables the configurations by setting <code>enabled</code> to <code>true</code>.
<pre>
resource "google_sql_database_instance" "database_instance_demo" {
  ...
  settings {
    backup_configuration {
      enabled = true
      ...
    }
  }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, GKE nodes run with Container-Optimized OS (COS). COS is an operating system image that is optimized to run GKE nodes on Google Compute Engine instances. Opting out of the default forgoes the benefits of enhanced security and efficiency.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example Terraform configuration sets up a pool of GKE nodes that do not run COS because <code>image_type</code> is set to a non-COS image in the <code>node_config</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_container_node_pool" "node_pool_demo" {
  ...
  node_config {
    image_type = "UBUNTU"
    ...
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Run GKE nodes with Google-managed COS images to ensure the operating system stays up-to-date with the best secure practices.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. GKE nodes use COS by setting <code>image_type</code> to <code>COS_CONTAINERD</code> in the <code>node_config</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_container_node_pool" "node_pool_demo" {
  ...
  node_config {
    image_type = "COS_CONTAINERD"
    ...
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, GKE automatically upgrades Kubernetes nodes to newer stable versions. Automatic upgrades, which ensure timely patching of known software vulnerabilities, are disabled.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example Terraform configuration disables automatic upgrades of Kubernetes nodes by setting <code>auto_upgrade</code> to <code>false</code> in the <code>management</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_container_node_pool" "node_pool_demo" {
...
  management {
    auto_upgrade = false
    ...
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Keep auto-upgrading nodes enabled.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It enables automatic upgrades of Kubernetes nodes by setting <code>auto_upgrade</code> to <code>true</code> in the <code>management</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_container_node_pool" "node_pool_demo" {
...
  management {
    auto_upgrade = true
    ...
  }
...
}
</pre>

Authorize the smallest practical IP address ranges access to the cluster control planes and block other IP addresses. If possible, only grant access to internal IP address ranges.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. In the <code>master_authorized_networks_config</code> block, the <code>cidr_block</code> argument defines an internal IP address range from which the control plane API endpoints accept connections.
<pre>
resource "google_container_cluster" "cluster_demo" {
  name = "name-demo"
  master_authorized_networks_config {
    cidr_blocks {
      cidr_block = "10.10.128.0/24"
      display_name = "10.network is private"
    }
  }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
A Kubernetes API server, the central management entity of a cluster, accepts HTTP basic authentication to authenticate users. HTTP Basic Authentication is deprecated and susceptible to brute force attacks and exposes user credentials to attackers in a misconfigured environment.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that enables HTTP basic authentication on a GKE cluster by setting <code>username</code> and <code>password</code> to non-empty values in the <code>master_auth</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_container_cluster" "container_cluster_demo" {
...
  master_auth {
    username = "foo"
    password = "bar"
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Do not use HTTP basic authentication, which was removed in GKE 1.19 and later. Use secure authentication mechanisms such as service account bearer tokens and OAuth tokens.

If a deprecated version of GKE is in use, set both the username and the password to an empty string to disable HTTP basic authentication. Otherwise, the Terraform configuration creates a cluster with a default user named <code>admin</code> for HTTP basic authentication.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It disables HTTP basic authentication by setting both <code>username</code> and <code>password</code> in the <code>master_auth</code> block to an empty string.
<pre>
resource "google_container_cluster" "container_cluster_demo" {
...
  master_auth {
    username = ""
    password = ""
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, the certificate-based authentication is disabled on new clusters that run GKE version 1.12 and later. With certificate-based authentication, a user presents a certificate that the Kubernetes API server verifies with the specified certificate authority. However, there is no way to revoke the certificate when the user leaves or loses the credentials. This makes the certificate-based authentication unsuitable for end users.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example Terraform configuration enables GKE client certificate-based authentication by setting <code>issue_client_certificate</code> to <code>true</code> in the <code>master_auth</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_container_cluster" "container_cluster_demo" {
...
  master_auth {
    client_certificate_config {
      issue_client_certificate = true
      ...
    }
    ...
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Do not enable client certificate-based authentication on a GKE cluster. Use secure authentication mechanisms such as service account bearer tokens and OAuth tokens.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It disables GKE client certificate-based authentication by setting <code>issue_client_certificate</code> to <code>false</code> in the <code>master_auth</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_container_cluster" "container_cluster_demo" {
...
  master_auth {
    client_certificate_config {
      issue_client_certificate = false
      ...
    }
    ...
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
DNSSEC prevents DNS spoofing by providing the ability to use digital signatures for DNS response validation. The DNSSEC of a Cloud DNS Domain is not enabled.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that disables DNSSEC on a Cloud DNS Domain by setting <code>state</code> to <code>off</code> in the <code>dnssec_config</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_dns_managed_zone" "zone-demo" {
...
  dnssec_config {
    state = "off"
    ...
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Enable DNSSEC on public Cloud DNS Domains.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It enables the DNSSEC of a Cloud DNS Domain by setting <code>state</code> to <code>on</code> in the <code>dnssec_config</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_dns_managed_zone" "zone-demo" {
...
  dnssec_config {
    state = "on"
    ...
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, you can use SSH keys stored in project metadata to access all Compute Engine instances in a project. This greatly expands the attack surface accessible to any compromised SSH key and violates the least privilege principle.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that permits project-wide SSH logins by setting <code>block-project-ssh-keys</code> to <code>false</code> in the <code>metadata</code> argument.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "compute_instance_demo" {
  ...
  metadata = {
    block-project-ssh-keys = false
    ...
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Block the use of project-wide SSH keys to access any Compute Engine instance. Grant access to Compute Engine instances on an as-needed basis.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It blocks project-wide SSH logins by setting <code>block-project-ssh-keys</code> to <code>true</code> in the <code>metadata</code> argument.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "compute_instance_demo" {
  ...
  metadata = {
    block-project-ssh-keys = true
    ...
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Failure to block unwanted network traffic expands a cloud service's attack surface. Services open to interaction with the public are subjected to almost continuous scanning and probing by malicious entities.

By default, the serial port of a Compute Engine instance is disabled for console access. Enabling serial console access can allow attackers to connect to the Compute Engine instance from any IP address because the serial port does not support IP-based access restrictions.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following Terraform configuration permits interactive serial console access by setting <code>serial-port-enable</code> to <code>true</code> in the <code>metadata</code> argument.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "compute_instance_demo" {
  ...
  metadata = {
    serial-port-enable = true
    ...
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Disable interactive serial console access to all Compute Engine instances.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It blocks serial console access by setting <code>serial-port-enable</code> to <code>false</code> in the <code>metadata</code> argument.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "compute_instance_demo" {
  ...
  metadata = {
    serial-port-enable = false
    ...
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

<b>Example 3:</b> The following example shows another way to resolve the issue in <code>Example 1</code>. The <code>compute.disableSerialPortAccess</code> organizational policy constraint disables serial console access for an entire organization.
<pre>
resource "google_organization_policy" "policy-demo" {
...
  constraint = "compute.disableSerialPortAccess"
...
  boolean_policy {
    enforced = true
  }
...
}
</pre>

Enable all recommended Shield VM options. Do not rely on the default, which might change.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It enables all recommended Shield VM options by setting the three identifiers to <code>true</code> in the <code>shielded_instance_config</code> block, namely <code>enable_integrity_monitoring</code>, <code>enable_secure_boot</code>, and <code>enable_vtpm</code>.

<pre>
resource "google_compute_instance" "compute_instance_demo" {
  ...
  shielded_instance_config {
    enable_integrity_monitoring = true
    enable_secure_boot = true
    enable_vtpm = true
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Every cloud service security feature is designed to prevent or mitigate a known threat. When disabled by intent or negligence, it offers no protection.

By default, if a GKE node repeatedly reports an unhealthy status over a given period, GKE initiates a repair process for that node. Disabling node auto-repair prevents timely and automated replacements of unhealthy nodes on which mission-critical workloads might run.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following Terraform configuration disables node auto-repair of a node pool by setting <code>auto_repair</code> to <code>false</code> in the <code>management</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_container_node_pool" "node-pool-demo" {
...
  management {
    auto_repair = false
    ...
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Ensure that node pools have auto-repair enabled.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It enables node auto-repair of a node pool by setting <code>auto_repair</code> to <code>true</code> in the <code>management</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_container_node_pool" "node-pool-demo" {
...
  management {
    auto_repair = true
    ...
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Failure to block unwanted network traffic expands a cloud service's attack surface. Services open to interaction with the public are subjected to almost continuous scanning and probing by malicious entities.

By default, Terraform deploys a Google Cloud SQL Database instance that only accepts connections from private IP addresses. Optional Authorized Networks settings define acceptable ranges of public IP addresses.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following Terraform configuration sets <code>value</code> to <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> in the <code>authorized_networks</code> block. A CIDR block of <code>/0</code> accepts connections from any IP address between 0.0.0.0 and 255.255.255.255.
<pre>
resource "google_sql_database_instance" "db-demo" {
  ...
  settings {
  ...
    ip_configuration {
    ...
      authorized_networks {
        name  = "any ip"
        value = "0.0.0.0/0"
      }
    ...
    }
    ...
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
On an as-needed basis, permit the smallest possible range of public IP addresses to connect to the Google Cloud SQL instance.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It permits a designated range of public IP addresses to connect to a Google Cloud SQL Database Instance by setting <code>value</code> to <code>192.0.2.0/24</code> in the <code>authorized_networks</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_sql_database_instance" "db-demo" {
  ...
  settings {
  ...
    ip_configuration {
    ...
      authorized_networks {
        name  = "TEST-NET-1"
        value = "192.0.2.0/24"
      }
    }
    ...
  }
  ...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Failure to block unwanted network traffic expands a cloud service's attack surface. Services open to interaction with the public are subjected to almost continuous scanning and probing by malicious entities.

Private GKE nodes do not have external IP addresses. Pods that run on these nodes cannot communicate beyond the cluster perimeter. Disabling a public endpoint protects the GKE cluster by limiting its management to internal private IP addresses. Failure to enforce a private endpoint and private nodes exposes the cluster to the public.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following Terraform configuration permits any public IP address to access cluster nodes and the control plane because <code>enable_private_nodes</code> and <code>enable_private_endpoint</code> are set to <code>false</code>.
<pre>
resource "google_container_cluster" "cluster-demo" {
...
  private_cluster_config {
    enable_private_endpoint = false
    enable_private_nodes = false
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Block any direct access to the cluster from an external IP address by default. Expose services to public IP addresses on an as-needed basis.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It blocks any client using an external IP address to connect to the control plane's public endpoint by setting <code>enable_private_endpoint</code> to <code>true</code>. In addition, setting <code>enable_private_nodes</code> to <code>true</code>, all cluster nodes can only have RFC 1918 private addresses. This strengthens security by making it difficult for an external host to establish a connection.
<pre>
resource "google_container_cluster" "cluster-demo" {
...
  private_cluster_config {
    enable_private_endpoint = true
    enable_private_nodes = true
  }
...
}
</pre>

Secure the domain for the App Engine application with SSL.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It contains an SSL configuration for the domain by defining an <code>ssl_settings</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_app_engine_domain_mapping" "domain_mapping" {
  domain_name = "example.com"

  ssl_settings {
    ssl_management_type = "AUTOMATIC"
  }

}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
A URL map is a set of rules to route incoming HTTP(S) requests to specific backend services. By default, a URL map accepts unencrypted and unsecured connections. These connections expose data to unauthorized access, potential theft, and tampering.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that defines a URL map that allows clients to use HTTP for communication by setting <code>https_redirect</code> to <code>false</code>.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_url_map" "urlmap" {
...
  default_url_redirect {
...
    https_redirect = false
...
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Send data over HTTPS to protect sensitive data in backend services. Redirect HTTP traffic to HTTPS.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. The URL map redirects traffic to HTTPS by setting <code>https_redirect</code> to <code>true</code>.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_url_map" "urlmap" {
...
  default_url_redirect {
...
    https_redirect = true
...
  }
...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
By default, Edge Cache services accept unencrypted and unsecured connections. These connections expose data to unauthorized access, potential theft, and tampering.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a Terraform configuration that defines an Edge Cache Service that allows clients to use HTTP for communication by setting <code>https_redirect</code> to <code>false</code>.
<pre>
resource "google_network_services_edge_cache_service" "srv_demo" {
...
  routing {
...
    path_matcher {
...
      route_rule {
...
        url_redirect {
          https_redirect = false
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Ensure Edge Cache serves sensitive data only through TLS-encrypted connections. Redirect all HTTP requests to HTTPS.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. The Edge Cache Service requires clients to use TLS for communication by setting <code>https_redirect</code> to <code>true</code>.
<pre>
resource "google_network_services_edge_cache_service" "srv_demo" {
...
  routing {
...
    path_matcher {
...
      route_rule {
...
        url_redirect {
          https_redirect = true
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
HTTPS uses TLS (Transport Layer Security) to encrypt regular HTTP requests and responses. It guarantees the integrity and confidentiality of any data exchange.

An HTTP Listener Connector does not encrypt its communication if its protocol is unspecified or set to <code>HTTP</code>. This exposes the data to unauthorized access, tampering, and potential theft.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following Mule configuration sets the <code>protocol</code> of an HTTP Listener Connector (<code>listener-connection</code>) to <code>HTTP</code>. As a result, connections to the HTTP Listener are insecure.
<pre>
    &lt;http:listener-config name=&quot;http_listener_config&quot;&gt;
        &lt;http:listener-connection host=&quot;example.com&quot; port=&quot;8080&quot; protocol=&quot;HTTP&quot;&gt;
...
        &lt;/http:listener-connection&gt;
    &lt;/http:listener-config&gt;
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Always use the <code>HTTPS</code> protocol, if available, for communication.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It enforces secure connections by setting the <code>protocol</code> of the HTTP Listener Connector (<code>listener-connection</code>) to <code>HTTPS</code>.
<pre>
    &lt;http:listener-config name=&quot;http_listener_config&quot;&gt;
        &lt;http:listener-connection host=&quot;example.com&quot; port=&quot;8080&quot; protocol=&quot;HTTPS&quot;&gt;
...
        &lt;/http:listener-connection&gt;
    &lt;/http:listener-config&gt;
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Global security requirements inform API consumers of the authentication and authorization parameters required to successfully interact with the API.

An absent global <code>security</code> definition might enable attackers to interact with sensitive API endpoints and allow them to perform operations that should be restricted to specific user accounts with specific privileges.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification neglects to declare a global <code>security</code> definition. APIs that implement this specification might be vulnerable to unauthorized or unauthenticated access to sensitive operations.
<pre>
{
    "openapi" : "3.0.3",
    "info" : {
        "title" : "My API",
        "version" : "1.0.0"
    },
    "servers" : [ {
        "url" : "https://example.org"
    } ],    
    ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
The global <code>security</code> definition provides the default authentication and authorization requirements for API operations.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines a global <code>security</code> definition.
<pre>
{
    "openapi" : "3.0.3",
    "info" : {
        "title" : "My API",
        "version" : "1.0.0"
    },
    "servers" : [ {
        "url" : "https://example.org"
    } ],
    "security" : [ {
        "oauth_auth" : [ "write:users", "read:users" ]
    } ],
    ...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
The <code>securitySchemes</code> definition specifies the security mechanisms that may be used globally or by specific API operations.

The <code>securitySchemes</code> definition is typically specified under the reusable <code>components</code> object and is referenced globally or by specific operations to dictate security requirements for interaction.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification fails to define the <code>securitySchemes</code> definition. 
<pre>
{
    "openapi" : "3.0.3",
    "info" : {
        "title" : "My API",
        "version" : "1.0.0"
    },   
    "components": {
        "schemas": {
        "GeneralError": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {            
            ...
            }
        }
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
The <code>securitySchemes</code> definition provides the required information for securely interacting with API endpoints.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It includes the <code>securitySchemes</code> definition.
<pre>
{
    "openapi" : "3.0.3",
    "info" : {
        "title" : "My API",
        "version" : "1.0.0"
    },
    "components": {
        "schemas": {
        "GeneralError": {
            "type": "object",            
            ...
        }
        "securitySchemes": {
            "api_key": {
                "type": "apiKey",
                "name": "api_key",
                "in": "header"
            },
        ...
        }
    }
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
API operations define security requirements to inform API consumers of the authentication and authorization parameters required to successfully invoke them.

Operations that do not include a <code>security</code> definition might enable attackers to interact with sensitive API endpoints and allow them to perform actions that should be restricted to specific user accounts with explicit privileges.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification fails to set a <code>security</code> definition for a sensitive operation. Additionally, without a global <code>security</code> definition, the <code>createUsers</code> operation is vulnerable to unauthorized and unauthenticated access.
<pre>
{
    "openapi": "3.0.0",
    "info": {
     ...
    },    
    "paths": {
      "/users": {
        "post": {
          "summary": "Create a user",
          "operationId": "createUsers",
         ...
        }
    ...
    }
}</pre>

Global security requirements inform API consumers of the authentication and authorization parameters required to successfully interact with the API.

An empty Global <code>security</code> definition might enable attackers to interact with sensitive API endpoints and allow them to perform operations that should be restricted to specific user accounts with specific privileges.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification declares an empty global <code>security</code> definition. APIs that implement this specification might be vulnerable to unauthorized or unauthenticated access to sensitive operations.
<pre>
{
    "openapi" : "3.0.3",
    "info" : {
        "title" : "My API",
        "version" : "1.0.0"
    },
    "servers" : [ {
        "url" : "/"
    } ],
    "security" : [],
    ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
The global <code>security</code> definition provides the default authentication and authorization requirements for API operations.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines a global <code>security</code> definition with an OAuth2 configuration.
<pre>
{
    "openapi" : "3.0.3",
    "info" : {
        "title" : "My API",
        "version" : "1.0.0"
    },
    "servers" : [ {
        "url" : "/"
    } ],
    "security" : [ {
        "oauth_auth" : [ "write:users", "read:users" ]
    } ],
    ...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Global security requirements inform API consumers of the authentication and authorization parameters required to successfully interact with the API.

Optional security requirement entries defined within the global <code>security</code> definition might enable attackers to interact with sensitive API endpoints and allow them to perform operations that should be restricted to specific user accounts with specific privileges.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification declares a global <code>security</code> definition with optional security via the empty <code>{}</code> item. APIs that implement this specification might be vulnerable to unauthorized or unauthenticated access to sensitive operations.
<pre>
{
    "openapi" : "3.0.3",
    "info" : {
        "title" : "My API",
        "version" : "1.0.0"
    },
    "servers" : [ {
        "url" : "/"
    } ],
     "security" : [ {}, { "oauth_auth" : ["write","read" ]} ],
    ...
}
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
The global <code>security</code> definition provides the default authentication and authorization requirements for API operations.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines a global <code>security</code> definition that has no optional entries.
<pre>
{
    "openapi" : "3.0.3",
    "info" : {
        "title" : "My API",
        "version" : "1.0.0"
    },
    "servers" : [ {
        "url" : "/"
    } ],
    "security" : [ {
        "oauth_auth" : [ "write:users", "read:users" ]
    } ],
    ...
}
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
API operations define security requirements to inform API consumers of the authentication and authorization parameters required to successfully invoke them.

Operations that include an empty <code>security</code> definition might enable attackers to interact with sensitive API endpoints and allow them to perform actions that should be restricted to specific user accounts with explicit privileges.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification sets an empty <code>security</code> definition for a sensitive operation. This overrides globally defined security requirements and renders the <code>createUsers</code> operation vulnerable to unauthorized and unauthenticated access.
<pre>
{
    "openapi": "3.0.0",
    "info": {
     ...
    },    
    "paths": {
      "/users": {
        "post": {
          "security": [],
          "summary": "Create a user",
          "operationId": "createUsers",
         ...
        }
    ...
    }
}</pre>

API operations define security requirements to inform API consumers of the authentication and authorization parameters required to successfully invoke them.

Operations that make security optional might enable attackers to interact with sensitive API endpoints and allow them to perform actions that should be restricted to specific user accounts with explicit privileges.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification makes security optional by including an empty object <code>{}</code> in the <code>security</code> definition for a sensitive operation. This overrides globally defined security requirements and renders the <code>createUsers</code> operation vulnerable to unauthorized and unauthenticated access.
<pre>
{
    "openapi": "3.0.0",
    "info": {
     ...
    },    
    "paths": {
      "/users": {
        "post": {
           "security": [
            {},
            {
              "my_auth": [
                "write:users"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "summary": "Create a user",
          "operationId": "createUsers",
         ...
        }
    ...
    }
}</pre>

Descriptions.Explanation:
By default, Azure Storage Accounts allow Storage Blob containers to enable anonymous access to their blobs.

Insufficient access configurations expose systems and broaden an organization's attack surface. Resources open to public access may unintentionally leak sensitive data.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an Azure Storage Account with the <code>allowBlobPublicAccess</code> property set to <code>true</code>. This allows its containers to enable public access of their blobs and data.
<pre>
{
    "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts",
    "apiVersion": "2021-04-01",
    "name": "[parameters('storageAccountName')]",
    "location": "[parameters('location')]",
    "sku": {
        "name": "Standard_LRS"
    },
    "kind": "StorageV2",
    "properties": {
        "accessTier": "Hot",
        "allowBlobPublicAccess": true
    }
}</pre>

By default, Azure storage accounts accept connections from clients on any network.

Loose access configurations unnecessarily expose systems and broaden an organization's attack surface. Services open to interaction with the public are subjected to almost continuous scanning and probing by malicious entities.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template fails to restrict network access to an Azure storage account.
<pre>
{
    ...
    "resources": [
        {
            "name": "VulnerableStorageAccount",
            "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts",
            "apiVersion": "2019-06-01",
            "tags": {
                "displayName": "vulnerablestore1"
            },
            "location": "[resourceGroup().location]",
            ...
            "properties": {
                "networkAcls": {
                    "bypass": "None",
                    "defaultAction": "Allow"
                }
            }
        }
    ],
    "outputs": {}
}</pre>

CloudWatch Logs provide a way to monitor CloudTrail logs for security-relevant events and raise alerts to concerned parties so they can take appropriate action.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following template defines an AWS trail without defining an appropriate <code>CloudWatchLogsLogGroupArn</code>.
<pre>
"myTrail": {
    "DependsOn": [
        "BucketPolicy"
    ],
    "Type": "AWS::CloudTrail::Trail",
    "Properties": {
        "S3BucketName": {
            "Ref": "S3Bucket"
        },
        "IsLogging": true,
    }
}
</pre>

By default, Amazon DocumentDB clusters do not deliver audit logs to Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an Amazon DocumentDB cluster without log monitoring.
<pre>
"Resources": {
    "DocDBDefault": {
        "Type": "AWS::DocDB::DBCluster",
        "Properties": {
            "MasterUsername": "name",
            "MasterUserPassword": "password",
            "StorageEncrypted": false
        }
    }
}</pre>

By default, Amazon Neptune clusters do not deliver audit logs to Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example shows a template that defines an Amazon Neptune cluster with no log monitoring.
<pre>
"Resources": {
    "MyNeptuneDB": {
        "Type": "AWS::Neptune::DBCluster",
        "Properties": {
            "DBClusterIdentifier": "DBClusterIdentifier"
        }
    },
}</pre>

By default, Amazon DocumentDB clusters enable transport encryption, but it can be disabled with the <code>tls</code> parameter.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an Amazon DocumentDB cluster with transport encryption disabled.
<pre>
"Resources": {
    "DocDBDisabledTLS": {
        "Type": "AWS::DocDB::DBClusterParameterGroup",
        "Properties": {
            "Description": "docdb cluster parameter group",
            "Name": "test",
            "Parameters": {
                "tls": "disabled",
                "ttl_monitor": "enabled"
            }
        }
    }
}
</pre>

By default, Azure AD Domain Services enables the use of known weak ciphers such as NTLMv1 and TLSv1.

Transport layer encryption (TLS) and authentication (NTLM) ciphers provide a protection mechanism to ensure the authenticity, confidentiality, and integrity of data transmitted between a client and web server.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example template defines an Azure Active Directory Domain Service with weak ciphers enabled.
<pre>
{

    "type": "Microsoft.AAD/domainServices",
    ...
    "properties": {
        "domainSecuritySettings": {
        "ntlmV1": "Enabled",
        "tlsV1": "Enabled"
        },
    }
    ...
}
</pre>

Granting <code>allUsers</code> or <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code> a BigQuery role gives anyone access to sensitive data.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example Terraform configuration grants all users (<code>allUsers</code>) the <code>roles/bigquery.dataOwner</code> role to access a BigQuery Dataset.
<pre>
resource "google_bigquery_dataset_iam_binding" "iam-binding-demo" {
    dataset_id = google_bigquery_dataset.dataset-demo.dataset_id
    role       = "roles/bigquery.dataViewer"
    members = [
        "allUsers"
    ]
}</pre>

Granting <code>allUsers</code> or <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code> a BigQuery role gives anyone access to sensitive data.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example Terraform configuration grants all users (<code>allUsers</code>) the <code>roles/bigquery.dataOwner</code> role to access a BigQuery Dataset.
<pre>
data "google_iam_policy" "policy-data-demo" {
    binding {
        role = "roles/bigquery.dataOwner"
        members = [
            "allUsers"
        ]
    }
}

resource "google_bigquery_dataset_iam_policy" "policy-demo" {
    dataset_id = "dataset-id-demo"
    policy_data = data.google_iam_policy.policy-data-demo.policy_data
}</pre>

Granting a BigQuery Dataset access for <code>allUsers</code> or <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code> gives anyone access to sensitive data.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following example Terraform configuration grants all users (<code>allUsers</code>) access to a BigQuery Dataset.
<pre>
resource "google_bigquery_dataset" "dataset-demo" {
    dataset_id = "dataset-id-demo"
    access {
        role   = "READER"
        special_group = "allUsers"
    }
}</pre>

An API operation has been detected that allows data exchange over an unencrypted channel. OpenAPI <code>security</code> requirements and target <code>servers</code> definitions for an API operation will always override the respective global settings. 

However, when these settings are not defined for an operation, they default to the globally specified values.

In this case, the operation explicitly defines target <code>servers</code> that contain a <code>url</code> configured with the <code>http</code> scheme.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification allows data to be transmitted over HTTP for the <code>greet</code> operation.
<pre>
{
  "openapi" : "3.0.3",
  "info" : {
   ...
  "paths" : {
    "/greet" : {
      "get" : {
        "servers": [
          {
              "url": "http://example.org",
          }
        ],
      ...
</pre>

An API operation has been detected that allows data exchange over an unencrypted channel. OpenAPI <code>security</code> requirements and target <code>servers</code> definitions for an API operation will always override the respective global settings. 

However, when these settings are not defined for an operation, they default to the globally specified values.

In this case, the operation does not define <code>security</code> requirements or target <code>servers</code> and inherits these settings from the global scope. The global scope defines target <code>servers</code> that contain a <code>url</code> with the <code>http</code> scheme.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification allows data to be transmitted over HTTP for the <code>greet</code> operation.
<pre>
{
  "openapi" : "3.0.3",
  "info" : {
   ...
  "servers": [
    {
        "url": "http://example.org",
    }
  ],
  "paths" : {
    "/greet" : {
      "get" : {
      ...
</pre>

In this instance, the operation requires the exchange of credentials specified in a <code>security</code> requirements block, and communication over HTTP is allowed by the target <code>servers</code> definition that contains a <code>url</code> that uses the <code>http</code> scheme.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification allows credentials to be transmitted over HTTP for the <code>greet</code> operation.
<pre>
{
  "openapi" : "3.0.3",
  "info" : {
   ...
  "paths" : {
    "/greet" : {
      "get" : {
        "security" : [ {
          "basicAuth": []
        } ],
        "servers": [
          {
              "url": "http://example.org",
          }
        ],
      ...
</pre>

In this instance, the operation does not define target <code>servers</code>, defaulting to the value specified globally. The global value defines target <code>servers</code> that contain a <code>url</code> that uses the <code>http</code> scheme, which exposes credentials specified by the operation's <code>security</code> requirements to eavesdropping, tampering, and theft. 

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification allows credentials to be transmitted over HTTP for the <code>greet</code> operation.
<pre>
{
  "openapi" : "3.0.3",
  "info" : {
   ...
  "servers": [
    {
        "url": "http://example.org",
    }
  ],
  "paths" : {
    "/greet" : {
      "get" : {
        "security" : [ {
          "basicAuth": []
        } ],
      ...
</pre>

In this instance, the operation does not define <code>security</code> requirements or target <code>servers</code>, defaulting to the values specified globally. The global specification defines target <code>servers</code> with a <code>url</code> that uses the <code>http</code> scheme, which exposes credentials specified in the global <code>security</code> requirements to eavesdropping, tampering, and theft. 

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification allows credentials to be transmitted over HTTP for the <code>greet</code> operation.
<pre>
{
  "openapi" : "3.0.3",
  "info" : {
   ...
  "security" : [ {
        "basicAuth": []
  } ],
  "servers": [
    {
        "url": "http://example.org",
    }
  ],
  "paths" : {
    "/greet" : {
      "get" : {
      ...
</pre>

In this instance, the operation defines target <code>servers</code> with a <code>url</code> that uses the <code>http</code> scheme. This overrides the globally specified value and exposes sensitive information to eavesdropping, tampering, and theft. The operation does not define <code>security</code> requirements and therefore inherits the value configured globally which requires the exchange of credentials.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification allows credentials to be transmitted over HTTP for the <code>greet</code> operation.
<pre>
{
  "openapi" : "3.0.3",
  "info" : {
   ...
  "security" : [ {
        "basicAuth": []
  } ],
  "servers": [
    {
        "url": "https://example.org",
    }
  ],
  "paths" : {
    "/greet" : {
      "get" : {
       "servers": [
          {
              "url": "http://example.org",
          }
        ],
      ...
</pre>

Basic Authentication involves sending client credentials in base64 encoding on each request to the server, which exposes the credentials to possible theft.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification defines Basic Authentication as the authentication scheme for API operations. 
<pre>
{
  "openapi": "3.0.0",
  ...
  "components": {
    ...
    "securitySchemes": {
      "basic_auth": {
        "scheme": "basic",
        "type": "http"
      },
      ...
}</pre>

API Key Authentication requires the client to keep a secret that is shared with the server. The key is sent on each request to the server, which exposes the key to possible theft.

API Key Authentication does not provide a way to determine the end user's identity, role, or privileges.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification allows API Key authentication.
<pre>
{
  "openapi": "3.0.0",
  ...
  "components": {
    ...
    "securitySchemes": {
      "apikey_auth": {
        "type": "apiKey",
        "name": "api_key",
        "in": "header"
      },
      ...
}</pre>

The <b>OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice</b> draft discourages the use of OAuth2 Implicit flow as it might allow token leakage and token replay attacks.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification defines an OAuth2 Implicit flow with PKCE. 
<pre>
{
  "openapi": "3.0.0",
  ...
  "components": {
    ...
    "securitySchemes": {
      "oauth_auth": {
        "type": "oauth2",
        "flows": {
          "implicit": {
            "authorizationUrl": "https://test.com/api/oauth/dialog",
            "scopes": {
              "write:users": "modify user profile",
            }
          },
          ...
        }
      }
    }
    ...
}</pre>

The <b>OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice</b> draft discourages the use of OAuth2 Resource Owner Password flow because it insecurely exposes user credentials to the client.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following OpenAPI specification defines an OAuth2 Resource Owner Password flow. 
<pre>
{
  "openapi": "3.0.0",
  ...
  "components": {
    ...
    "securitySchemes": {
      "oauth_auth": {
        "type": "oauth2",
        "flows": {
          "password": {
            "tokenUrl": "https://test.com/api/oauth/dialog",
            "scopes": {
              "write:users": "modify user profile",
            }
          },
          ...
        }
      }
    }
    ...
}</pre>

In this case, the operation inherits a global security definition but does not define the <code>403</code> status code response.

