Descriptions.Recommendations:
Do not allow storage accounts to have any publicly accessible data to reduce the risk of unintentional exposure of sensitive data.

Review your organization's data handling policies when creating resources that are readable by anonymous users.

When making a change to access restrictions, identify all dependent systems to limit potential disruptions.

Note that the <code>allowBlobPublicAccess</code> property is only available for templates with <code>apiVersion</code> 2019-04-01 or later.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines an Azure Storage Account with the <code>allowBlobPublicAccess</code> property set to <code>false</code>, which allows authorized access only and prevents containers from enabling public access to their blobs.
<pre>
{
    "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts",
    "apiVersion": "2021-04-01",
    "name": "[parameters('storageAccountName')]",
    "location": "[parameters('location')]",
    "sku": {
        "name": "Standard_LRS"
    },
    "kind": "StorageV2",
    "properties": {
        "accessTier": "Hot",
        "allowBlobPublicAccess": false
    }
}</pre>

Restrict critical services access to specific IP addresses, ranges, or virtual networks.

Configure Azure Storage firewall rules to block all traffic by default and specify the particular sources of trusted communication.

To specify trusted sources, configure the <code>networkAcls/ipRules</code>, <code>networkAcls/resourceAccessRules</code> or <code>networkAcls/virtualNetworkRules</code>. Then, to block all traffic by default, configure the <code>networkAcls/defaultAction</code> property to <code>Deny</code>.

Note that the <code>networkAcls</code> property was introduced in <code>apiVersion</code> 2017-06-01 and later.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It restricts network access to an Azure storage account to a specific virtual network.
<pre>
{
...
"resources": [
        {
            "name": "VulnerableStorageAccount",
            "type": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts",
            "apiVersion": "2019-06-01",
            "tags": {
                "displayName": "vulnerablestore1"
            },
            "location": "[resourceGroup().location]",
            ...
            "properties": {
                "networkAcls": {
                    "bypass": "None",
                    "virtualNetworkRules": [
                        {
                        "id": "[variables('subnetId')[0]]",
                        "action": "Allow"
                        }
                    ],
                    "defaultAction": "Deny"
                }
            }
        }
    ],
    "outputs": {}
}</pre>

Amazon DocumentDB can export various types of logs including: system profiling logs, audit logs, and instance event logs.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It defines the <code>EnableCloudwatchLogsExports</code> property that specifies the types of logs to be exported.
<pre>
"Resources": {
    "DocDBProfileLogExport": {
        "Type": "AWS::DocDB::DBCluster",
        "Properties": {
            "MasterUsername": "name",
            "MasterUserPassword": "password",
            "StorageEncrypted": false,
            "EnableCloudwatchLogsExports": [
                "profiler"
            ]
        }
    },
    "DocDBAuditLogExport": {
        "Type": "AWS::DocDB::DBCluster",
        "Properties": {
            "MasterUsername": "name",
            "MasterUserPassword": "password",
            "StorageEncrypted": false,
            "EnableCloudwatchLogsExports": [
                "audit"
            ]
        }
    },
}</pre>

Specify the VPC Flow log options in the <code>log_config</code> block.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It specifies VPC Flow log options in the <code>log_config</code> block.
<pre>
resource "google_compute_subnetwork" "subnetwork1" {
    name = "name-demo"
    ip_cidr_range = "10.2.0.0/16"
    network = google_compute_network.network1.self_link
    log_config {
        metadata = "INCLUDE_ALL_METADATA"
    }
}
</pre>

Avoid granting any BigQuery roles to <code>allUsers</code> or <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code>. Assign specific users or groups to a BigQuery role on an as-needed basis.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It grants a BigQuery role to a BigQuery Dataset for a group of users identified by email addresses.
<pre>
resource "google_bigquery_dataset_iam_binding" "iam-binding-demo" {
    dataset_id = google_bigquery_dataset.dataset-demo.dataset_id
    role       = "roles/bigquery.dataViewer"
    members = [
        "user:admin@example.com","user:soc@example.com"
    ]
}</pre>

Avoid granting any BigQuery roles to <code>allUsers</code> or <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code>. Assign specific users or groups to a BigQuery role on an as-needed basis.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It grants access to a BigQuery Dataset for a group of users identified by email addresses.
<pre>
data "google_iam_policy" "policy-data-demo" {
    binding {
        role = "roles/bigquery.dataOwner"
        members = [
            "admin@example.com", "soc@example.com"
        ]
    }
}

resource "google_bigquery_dataset_iam_policy" "policy-demo" {
    dataset_id = "dataset-id-demo"
    policy_data = data.google_iam_policy.policy-data-demo.policy_data
}</pre>

Avoid granting any access to <code>allUsers</code> or <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code>. Assign specific users or groups to a BigQuery role on an as-needed basis.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following example fixes the problem in <code>Example 1</code>. It grants access to a BigQuery Dataset for a group of users identified by an email address (<code>group_by_email</code>).
<pre>
resource "google_bigquery_dataset" "dataset-demo" {
    dataset_id = "dataset-id-demo"
    access {
        role   = "READER"
        group_by_email = "admin@example.com"
    }
}</pre>

Consider using a more secure authentication scheme such as OAuth2.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following OpenAPI specification allows only OAuth2 for authentication.
<pre>
{
  "openapi": "3.0.0",
  ...
  "components": {
    ...
    "securitySchemes": {
      "oauth_auth": {
        "type": "oauth2"
        "flows": {
          "authorizationCode": {
            "authorizationUrl": "http://tes.org/api/oauth/dialog",
            "scopes": {
              "read:users": "read user profiles",
           ...
}</pre>

